      "answer": "The customer's issue that started after the 2026-02-20 taxonomy rollout is BlueHarbor Logistics. They reported search relevance degradation and increased manual triage time following the taxonomy change on 2026-02-20.\n\nThe proof plan proposed to get BlueHarbor Logistics comfortable with renewal involved a short-term mitigation consisting of updating index weighting and adding a translation/mapping layer to map new taxonomy terms to historical query tokens for the top 20 saved searches. The timeline included receiving schema registry export and query logs by 2026-03-19, starting an A/B test in staging on 2026-03-22 for 5 business days tuning plus 2 business days validation (7-10 business days total). The acceptance criteria were top-5 correct hit rate >= 80%, no regression in suppression logic, and incident correlation latency under 200ms.\n\nA 90-day professional services engagement was offered at $48k fixed price to implement the tuning, build the mapping layer, and run the proof, with a 30% discount if Orchestrator seat expansion was signed concurrently. Additionally, a conditional credit clause was proposed: if the proof does not meet the agreed top-5 accuracy uplift within 10 business days, a one-quarter invoice credit prorated against the renewal for the upcoming year would be applied.\n\nThe proof plan also included compatibility tests against existing EN-RULES-ENGINE automations to ensure no breakage, and a 1-day production rollout and 72-hour monitoring window if validation passed.\n\nNo other customer's issue starting after the 2026-02-20 taxonomy rollout or other proof plans for renewal comfort besides BlueHarbor Logistics are documented in the evidence [art_8b0063fbb3cb, art_0bccc580184e, art_bd3560dfe194, art_c9970c1dc932].",
